    formulaExplanation: "Escape velocity is the minimum speed an object needs to break free of a body's gravity without further propulsion, and it comes from setting the object's kinetic energy equal to the gravitational potential energy binding it. Solving that energy balance gives the square root of twice the gravitational constant times the body's mass divided by its radius. The formula works because a more massive body pulls harder and requires more speed, while starting farther from the centre, at a larger radius, weakens the pull and lowers the escape speed. Notably it does not depend on the escaping object's own mass.",
    explanation: "Escape velocity is the minimum speed an object must reach to break free of a planet, moon, or star's gravitational pull and never fall back, without any additional thrust. This escape velocity calculator computes it from the celestial body's mass and radius using Newton's law of gravitation. For Earth this speed is about 11.2 kilometres per second, roughly 33 times the speed of sound, which is why launching into space demands such enormous rockets.\n\nThis concept matters for spaceflight, astronomy, and planetary science. Mission planners use escape velocity to work out the energy a spacecraft needs to leave Earth or land on and depart from another world. It also explains why small bodies like the Moon cannot hold onto an atmosphere, since gas molecules easily exceed the low escape speed, while massive planets like Jupiter retain thick atmospheres. A black hole, in the extreme, has an escape velocity exceeding the speed of light.\n\nThe formula works by balancing kinetic energy against gravitational potential energy, so escape velocity rises with the body's mass and falls as the launch radius increases. Remarkably, it does not depend on the mass of the escaping object, so a pebble and a spacecraft need the same speed. Astronomers, engineers, and physics students rely on an escape velocity calculator to compare worlds and plan the energy budgets of space missions.",
