    formula: "approaching: f' = f · c/(c − vₛ)   ·   receding: f' = f · c/(c + vₛ)",
    formulaExplanation: "The Doppler effect changes the frequency an observer hears because a moving source compresses the sound waves ahead of it and stretches those behind it. When the source approaches, each successive wave crest is emitted a little closer, so the waves bunch up, the wavelength shortens, and the observed frequency rises; dividing by (c minus the source speed) captures this compression. When the source recedes, the crests spread out and the frequency drops, so the formula divides by (c plus the source speed). The speed of sound sets how fast the waves travel relative to the moving source.",
    explanation: "The Doppler effect is the change in the frequency of a wave that an observer detects when the source is moving relative to them. This Doppler effect calculator computes the observed frequency for sound from a moving source and a stationary observer, using the source frequency, the source's speed, the speed of sound, and whether the source is approaching or receding. It is the physics behind the familiar rise and fall in pitch of a passing siren or racing car.\n\nThis matters well beyond everyday sound. Doppler radar tracks the motion of storms and speeding vehicles, medical ultrasound measures blood flow, and astronomers use the Doppler shift of light to discover that distant galaxies are receding, evidence that the universe is expanding. Understanding how motion shifts frequency lets engineers and scientists turn a pitch or colour change into a precise measurement of speed.\n\nThe formula works because a source moving toward you emits each wave crest from a slightly closer position, bunching the waves into a higher frequency, while a receding source stretches them into a lower one. Selecting the direction tells the calculator whether to divide by the speed of sound minus or plus the source speed. Physics students, engineers, and anyone curious about that shifting siren pitch rely on a Doppler effect calculator to quantify exactly how much the frequency changes.",
